title: Believers Cannot Apostatize and Apostates Cannot Believe
description: Men who know the truth and hold it in unrighteousness are given up to a reprobate mind; a true believer is kept by the power of God, and those who fall away were never saved.
output: lessons/heresies/believers-cannot-apostatize.html

# From studies/data/notes/believers-cannot-apostatize-notes.html, in the author's words, arranged into slides.
# Spelling fixed: apostasize -> apostatize, vein -> vain, let away -> led away, depart for me -> depart from me,
# TL:DR -> TL;DR, She had full knowledge -> He (Naaman), Arianism -> Arminianism (the lose-salvation view, as the
# perseverance-of-the-saints notes name it). "Not all heresies for someone to become apostates ..." has "cause" supplied
# looked to have lost a word; "cause" is supplied. The quoted verses are pulled from the KJV by the builder.

=== Romans 1
--- Believers Cannot Apostatize and Apostates Cannot Believe [title]
overline: Answering error
heading: Believers Cannot Apostatize and Apostates Cannot Believe
sub: Believers cannot apostatize and apostates cannot believe.
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move through the slides. Tap any gold reference to read the verse.

--- Without excuse
scripture: Romans 1:18-23 | mark: who hold the truth in unrighteousness; so that they are without excuse

--- Man has no excuse
lead: That which may be known of God is made manifest by His wrath revealed from Heaven. Man has no excuse.
p: Many have this understanding, the law written in their hearts and the conviction of the Spirit, and the wonder of creation and even the example of mercy towards the saved believer...and still don't repent towards the gospel, perishing in their sins.

--- Given up to a reprobate mind
p: But these people being described know the truth of God and hold it in unrighteousness. They do not wish to retain God in their knowledge. They reject the truth for a lie. They worship themselves and the created over the Creator. And God gives them up to a reprobate (rejected) mind.
callout: For those that not only had the manifest truth of God, but a clear articulation of the gospel of grace and reconciliation through faith in Christ Jesus, understand it, and even profess to assent to it, and then reject it? They are worse off than those that simply never hear or consider it.

=== The apostates
--- These have forsaken the right way
lead: These have forsaken the right way;
big: 2 Peter 2:17

--- Twice-dead
lead: The apostates are reprobate, twice-dead. Not only dead and hellbound in the next world, but conscience seared and accursed in this one.
big: 2 Peter 2:21

--- Strong delusion
p: But we who have trusted the truth in our hearts have a more sure hope. We have the Spirit of truth to defend us from the spirit of error. Jesus said it is impossible to fool the elect. God gives them strong delusions as punishment for their rejection of the truth.
scripture: 2 Thessalonians 2:10-12 | mark: strong delusion

=== Kept from falling
--- Not possible for a believer
lead: But praise God this is not possible for a believer.
p: We are kept from falling by the power of God. By the witness of the Spirit. By the perfect work of Christ, knowing we have not believed in vain.
big: Jude 1:24-25

--- They went out from us
p: Regarding the lie that those who are saved CAN fall away, can believe a strong delusion, can be fooled, the Bible teaches this is not possible.
scripture: 1 John 2:18-21 | mark: They went out from us, but they were not of us

--- Ye shall abide in him
kicker: 1 John 2
scripture: 1 John 2:22-27 | mark: ye shall abide in him

--- Try the spirits
p: John continues;
scripture: 1 John 4:1-6 | mark: Hereby know we the spirit of truth, and the spirit of error

--- The unction of the holy Spirit
lead: This is the unction of the holy Spirit. This Spirit of truth. That we hear those of God and reject those heard of the world by their false teaching.
refs: 1 John 2:20; 1 John 2:27; 1 John 4:6

--- The sheep hear his voice
scripture: John 10:1-5 | mark: And a stranger will they not follow

--- We will not follow the stranger
lead: We who are saved WILL NOT FOLLOW the stranger. We hear Jesus' voice. This is the promise of God.
scripture: John 10:26-30 | mark: they shall never perish

=== Impossible
--- It is impossible
lead: It is impossible for a saved child of God to fall away into apostasy. The trust in a false way, after knowing and assenting to the truth. And it is impossible for an apostate to ever be saved once they've rejected the truth.
scripture: Hebrews 6:4-6 | mark: For it is impossible

--- It is heresy
panel-warn: It's heresy | Anyone teaching that saved believers can reject the truth, believe a lie, have strong delusion, and apostatize, is wrong. It's heresy. It is not possible.
panel: Nor the reverse | Just as it is not possible for one who went out from us, who held the truth in unrighteousness and didn't wish to retain God in their knowledge, to ever come to the knowledge of the truth.

=== Heresy is not apostasy
--- False prophets and false teachers
p: The Bible warns repeatedly against false prophets, false teachers who try to lead away people with itching ears by their vain jangling. Creeping unawares in order to spy out our liberty, and to make merchandise of us.
p: Just like Satan, they try to make you doubt God's word in order to get you to sin. Or to believe foolishness. A saved believer can be led away into believing some heresies that are couched in the truth.
callout: These heresies are not necessarily apostasy. In order for it to be apostasy, it has to change the truth of God into a lie. Not all heresies are damnable. Not all heresies cause someone to become apostate by adopting them as a school of thought or a systematic that they apply to their existing faith.

--- Backslidden, not lost
p: Nor does a heart that is no longer perfect towards God, but has added other elements of worship into their true faith, make someone an apostate. Yes they are not right and yes they are backslidden and yes they will face the chastening of God, but they have not lost their salvation.
note: Theologians call adding falsehoods or heresies to true faith syncretism.

--- Solomon
p: Solomon was guilty of this. He never rejected the Lord God or the temple he was given to build or the covenant and sacrifices that represented the Christ to come. But his heart was turned away to other gods by the love of his wives, partaking in their idolatry in addition to the truth he held in his heart.
p: This applies to saved believers who use terms like repent of your sins, or who believe in some of the elements of Calvinism and predestination. Or who get caught up in other false doctrines, or never fully abandon the religion that they were won out of.
p: It may apply to someone who trusted in Christ at the door when a soul winner came and presented the gospel to them, but out of habit and loyalty and family ties they continue on going to a Catholic Church for example.

--- Naaman
p: Naaman was a Syrian general who asked the Lord for forgiveness for entering an idol's temple with his king, after believing on the truth of God.
big: 2 Kings 5:18

--- Never trust the false gospel
p: He had full knowledge of what he was doing and understood that it was wrong. I would assume Solomon did too, knowing the punishment he received through his son and the divided kingdom for his spiritual adultery.
p: But there are thousands if not more so of saved believers who never continue to walk in the spirit and read the Bible and learn God's ways who simply went back to their Catholic church or their Mormon church or whatever apostate cult they were in, and while it may prick them in the heart they might not recognize why.
callout: But what they'll never do is trust in the false gospel of that church. Just as Naaman never trusted in the false gods of his people ever again.

=== The current heresy
--- Creeping up
p: And I think it's curious that this current heresy that a saved believer could apostatize and could believe a false gospel in their heart, or reject the truth of God utterly, has been creeping up over the past few years.
p: It's a twist on Arminianism, which holds that a saved believer can become apostate and lose salvation. By adding the assurance of salvation to it, it allows them to lead away babes in Christ or people who aren't fully trusting the truth, and give them cover to chase after delusions.

--- What it does
panel: To the believer | A believer they lead away with this vain jangling won't lose their salvation obviously. They also won't come to believe a lie ultimately because they can't. But they'll introduce doubt and they'll give a bad testimony and when the false teacher can get baby Christians to slander men of God, soul-winning efforts, the teaching of Truth, hurt the cause of Christ.
panel-warn: To the nominal Christian | But a nominal Christian, someone who has not fully believed unto salvation, it allows them to continue in whatever lie they are holding on to to avoid repentance. And it softens them up to be led away by whatever other strange doctrine the false teacher decides to impart upon their proselytes. He makes them twofold more the child of Hell than themselves.

--- A Trojan horse
lead: These false teachers are attempting to have it both ways.
p: They use the comforting banner of "Once Saved, Always Saved" as a Trojan horse to smuggle in a license for total apostasy, claiming a person can completely deny Christ, turn to paganism, or become an atheist, and that God will simply override their will and force them to believe in the end.

--- It collapses under Scripture
lead: This teaching collapses under the weight of Scripture for several critical reasons.
point: Faith redefined | It redefines Faith as a meaningless past transaction, that purchased salvation, Rather than treating belief as a state of being. It becomes a transient action in their minds, rather than the reality their heart would live in if they believe the truth.
point: No forcing of faith | Of course we reject Calvinism, so there is no forcing of faith or salvation onto someone who has utterly rejected it. The Bible says they're ever learning and never able to come to the knowledge of the truth. Jesus says depart from me I never knew you.
refs: 2 Timothy 3:7; Matthew 7:23

--- No third option
point: A class the Bible doesn't name | It invents a class of people that the Bible doesn't say exists. There is a binary of those who believe and those who are condemned already because they have not believed. There is no third option.
point: The Holy Spirit overlooked | They purposefully overlook the power of the Holy Spirit and His purpose, to seal the believer, guide us to all truth and show the spirit of error.
note: While that doesn't rise to the level of blaspheming the Holy Spirit, in my opinion, it sure does come close.
refs: John 3:18

--- It speaks for itself
p: And finally, the fact that someone who has engaged in teaching this false doctrine and slandering great men of God over this heresy, Is literally suffering a psychotic breakdown with satanic visions and delusions of grandeur, profaning Christ by claiming to have the power and purpose of Christ, speaks for itself.
callout: And those vipers who delight in the strong delusions they helped sow, laughing and encouraging it, are wicked.

--- TL;DR [title]
overline: TL;DR
heading: Believers cannot apostatize and apostates cannot believe

=== Hebrews 6
--- The parable
scripture: Hebrews 6:7-9 | mark: whose end is to be burned
p: Here, the Bible gives us the parable to explain the apostasy verses that came before it.

--- Herbs and thorns
p: The same rains and earth are given to both the dressed fields that bring forth herbs also brings forth thorns and briers, rejected and cast into the fire.
p: This directly applies to those that tasted of the truth, but did not consume, who see the power of the Spirit but never trusted the truth. They are the thorns and briers. The saved are the herbs meet for the dresser (the fruit of those who won them to Christ).
callout: The ground that bears thorns isn't good soil that "lost" its status; it is bad ground whose true nature was finally exposed by the rain.

--- Better things of you
lead: The parable explains the concept beautifully.
p: It does not say the herbs become thorns, let alone that briers eventually are turned to fruit.
p: And so Paul, having explained this concept, reassures the believers he is writing to.
big: Hebrews 6:9

=== 2 Timothy 2
--- If we believe not
p: Others use 2 Timothy 2 to say that a believer can fall away from the faith.
big: 2 Timothy 2:13

--- The surrounding context
kicker: 2 Timothy 2:8-14
scripture: 2 Timothy 2:8-14 | mark: if we deny him, he also will deny us

--- The unchanging gospel
p: The passage is not primarily about individual apostasy of previously saved believers. It forms part of Paul’s larger instruction to men of God regarding sound doctrine, endurance, and the unchanging nature of the gospel.
p: The statement that God remains faithful even if “we believe not” underscores the immutability of God’s character, His word, and His decrees. The truth of the gospel does not depend on human belief; it stands regardless of whether people accept it.

--- He cannot deny himself
p: Immediately preceding verse 13 is the conditional warning of verse 12: “if we deny him, he also will deny us.” Isolating verse 13 while setting aside this earlier clause produces an incomplete reading.
p: Verse 13 does not teach that a person who once believed and later ceases to believe is nevertheless guaranteed heaven by God’s faithfulness. Rather, it affirms that God cannot deny Himself—His own character, gospel, and justice. If God has decreed judgment upon those who deny Him and remain in unbelief, He remains faithful to that decree.
refs: 2 Timothy 2:12; 2 Timothy 2:13

--- Apostasia
p: The claim that the Greek term *apostasia* (“a falling away” or “departure”) can apply only to those who were genuinely saved represents a significant logical leap. Departure can occur from a visible community, a theological position, or a religious system without the individual ever having possessed the corresponding spiritual reality.
p: Judas Iscariot provides a clear illustration: those who “went out from us” demonstrated by their departure that “they were not of us.” Visual or institutional departure does not prove prior genuine possession of saving faith.
refs: 1 John 2:19

--- Not a safety net
p: An interpretation that treats verse 13 as a permanent safety net for later unbelief or apostasy produces a distorted picture of God—one who would compel a hostile, self-professed atheist into heaven solely on the basis of a past intellectual assent.
p: Such a view reduces the blood of Christ to something closer to a mechanical formula than a transformative covenant, resembling the very “sinner’s prayer” approach that is often criticized for treating salvation as a mere verbal transaction rather than a genuine work of grace.

=== Close
--- Believers cannot apostatize
point: Believers are kept | We are kept from falling by the power of God. By the witness of the Spirit. By the perfect work of Christ, knowing we have not believed in vain.
point: Apostates cannot believe | It is impossible for an apostate to ever be saved once they've rejected the truth.
point: Heresy is not apostasy | In order for it to be apostasy, it has to change the truth of God into a lie.
